# Copy to .env. Never commit it: it holds tool credentials.
#
# The release to run: a version, or better its digest (see README.md). There is no default, because `latest` moves on
# every release and `edge` on every merge. The first release that carries the gateway image publishes its version tag;
# until then use `edge` to try it, not in production.
AGENT_CUSTODY_GATEWAY_VERSION=
# Where the port is published. 127.0.0.1 keeps it on this host; set 0.0.0.0 only behind your own TLS and network controls.
GATEWAY_BIND=127.0.0.1
GATEWAY_PORT=8790
# For the tls profile: a DNS name that points at this host.
# GATEWAY_HOST=gateway.example.com
# Credentials the gateway's upstreams read (the names come from `headerEnv` and `tokenEnv` in gateway.json).
# MY_API_TOKEN=Bearer ...
# AGENT_CUSTODY_LOG_TOKEN=...
# The user the gateway, the console, and init run as, so that what one writes the other can read (the gateway's key is mode 0600).
# The default is the image's own user; ./config must be writable by it for the console to save a policy (sudo chown 10001:10001 config).
# To use your own ids instead, set them here and also set GATEWAY_DATA to a directory you own, because the default data volume belongs to 10001.
# GATEWAY_UID=10001
# GATEWAY_GID=10001
# GATEWAY_DATA=./data
# For the console profile: where its published port is, and which file holds its sign-in token. The token is a Docker secret that only
# the console service reads. It does not go in this file, which the gateway reads. Make it with: openssl rand -hex 32 > console-token && chmod 600 console-token
# CONSOLE_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CONSOLE_PORT=8795
# CONSOLE_TOKEN_FILE=./console-token
